Task 1: Add RHVH Instance to RHVM
Subtask 1.1: Prepare the RHVH Host
First, we need to ensure the RHVH host is properly configured and ready for registration.

Step 1: Access the RHVH Host
Connect to your RHVH host using SSH:

ssh root@rhvh-host-ip
Step 2: Verify Network Connectivity
Test connectivity to the RHVM server:

ping -c 4 rhvm-server-ip
Expected output should show successful ping responses.

Step 3: Check RHVH Service Status
Verify that essential RHVH services are running:

systemctl status vdsmd
systemctl status libvirtd
systemctl status ovirt-imageio-daemon
All services should show active (running) status.

Step 4: Configure Firewall Rules
Ensure necessary ports are open for RHVM communication:

firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=ovirt-imageio
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=ovirt-storageconsole
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=ovirt-vmconsole
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=54321/tcp
firewall-cmd --reload
Subtask 1.2: Register RHVH Using Web Console
Step 1: Access RHVM Web Administration Portal
Open your web browser
Navigate to: https://rhvm-server-ip/ovirt-engine
Accept the SSL certificate warning if prompted
Log in with administrator credentials:
Username: admin
Password: [provided in lab environment]
Domain: internal
Step 2: Navigate to Hosts Section
Click on Compute in the left navigation menu
Select Hosts from the dropdown menu
Click the New button to add a new host
Step 3: Configure Host Registration Details
Fill in the New Host dialog with the following information:

General Tab:

Name: rhvh-host-01
Comment: Primary RHVH Host for Lab Environment
Hostname: rhvh-host-ip (use the actual IP address)
Password: [root password for RHVH host]
Advanced Parameters:

SSH Port: 22
SSH Fingerprint: Leave blank (will be auto-detected)
Step 4: Configure Host Cluster Assignment
In the General tab, locate the Host Cluster dropdown
Select Default cluster (or create a new cluster if needed)
Ensure Data Center is set to Default
Step 5: Initiate Host Registration
Click OK to begin the registration process
Monitor the progress in the Events tab at the bottom of the screen
The registration process typically takes 5-10 minutes
Subtask 1.3: Register RHVH Using Command Line Interface
As an alternative method, you can also register the host using CLI commands.

Step 1: Access RHVM Server via SSH
ssh root@rhvm-server-ip
Step 2: Use ovirt-engine-cli Tool
Install the CLI tool if not already present:

dnf install ovirt-engine-cli -y
Step 3: Create Host Registration Script
Create a script to automate host registration:

cat > register_host.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

# RHVM connection details
RHVM_URL="https://localhost/ovirt-engine/api"
RHVM_USER="admin@internal"
RHVM_PASS="your_admin_password"

# Host details
HOST_NAME="rhvh-host-01"
HOST_ADDRESS="rhvh-host-ip"
HOST_PASSWORD="rhvh_root_password"
CLUSTER_NAME="Default"

# Register the host
ovirt-shell -c -u "$RHVM_USER" -p "$RHVM_PASS" -U "$RHVM_URL" << SHELL_EOF
add host --name "$HOST_NAME" --address "$HOST_ADDRESS" --root_password "$HOST_PASSWORD" --cluster-name "$CLUSTER_NAME"
SHELL_EOF

echo "Host registration initiated for $HOST_NAME"
EOF
Step 4: Execute Registration Script
Make the script executable and run it:

chmod +x register_host.sh
./register_host.sh
Task 2: Verify Host Registration Status
Subtask 2.1: Check Registration Status via Web Console
Step 1: Monitor Host Status in RHVM
Return to the RHVM web console
Navigate to Compute → Hosts
Locate your newly registered host rhvh-host-01
Observe the Status column
Step 2: Understand Status Indicators
The host will progress through several states:

Installing: Initial setup and package installation
Reboot: Host is rebooting after installation
Non Operational: Host is starting services
Up: Host is fully operational and ready
Step 3: View Detailed Host Information
Click on the host name rhvh-host-01
Review the General tab for basic information
Check the Hardware tab for system specifications
Examine the Network Interfaces tab for network configuration
Subtask 2.2: Verify Registration Using Command Line
Step 1: Check Host Status from RHVM Server
Connect to RHVM server and check host status:

ssh root@rhvm-server-ip
Query the database for host information:

su - postgres -c "psql engine -c \"SELECT name, status, cluster_id FROM vds WHERE name='rhvh-host-01';\""
Step 2: Verify Services on RHVH Host
Connect to the RHVH host and verify services:

ssh root@rhvh-host-ip
Check VDSM service status:

systemctl status vdsmd
journalctl -u vdsmd -f
Step 3: Test VDSM Communication
Verify VDSM is responding to RHVM requests:

vdsm-client Host getCapabilities
This command should return detailed host capabilities in JSON format.

Subtask 2.3: Troubleshoot Common Registration Issues
Issue 1: SSH Connection Failures
If registration fails due to SSH issues:

# On RHVH host, check SSH service
systemctl status sshd
systemctl restart sshd

# Verify SSH key authentication
ssh-keygen -R rhvh-host-ip
Issue 2: Firewall Blocking Communication
Check and configure firewall rules:

# On RHVH host
firewall-cmd --list-all
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="rhvm-server-ip" accept'
firewall-cmd --reload
Issue 3: Certificate Issues
Resolve SSL certificate problems:

# On RHVH host
rm -rf /etc/pki/vdsm/certs/*
systemctl restart vdsmd
